 ## Overview
-Path Replay Backpropagation (PRB) is an adjoint-based differentiable rendering method that computes gradients of a scalar loss function with respect to scene parameters without storing the full Monte Carlo computation graph [S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the quadratic runtime complexity of its sibling Radiative Backpropagation (RB) by using a two-pass approach: a primal pass to generate samples and store state, followed by an adjoint pass that replays the path using the same random seed to recover incident radiance [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike RB, which requires an intertwined simulation of adjoint and primal radiance leading to quadratic complexity, PRB achieves linear time complexity in path length [S3, unofficial].
+Path Replay Backpropagation (PRB) is an adjoint-based differentiable rendering method that computes gradients of a scalar loss function with respect to scene parameters without storing the full Monte Carlo computation graph [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It solves the quadratic runtime complexity of its sibling Radiative Backpropagation (RB) by using a two-pass approach: a primal pass to generate samples and store state, followed by an adjoint pass that replays the path using the same random seed to recover incident radiance [S1][S3, unofficial]. Unlike RB, which requires an intertwined simulation of adjoint and primal radiance leading to quadratic complexity, PRB achieves linear time complexity in path length [S3, unofficial].
 
